From: merlin
Subject: [0107] GPU user client answers backboardd's selector 2

The 0106 boot log (research/logs/sweetlog-0106-20261030.txt) shows the
whole story: backboardd matched AGXAccelerator, IOServiceOpen type 1
succeeded (-> 0x0), and it asked exactly ONE selector - sel 2, 0 bytes
in, 536 bytes out - got kIOReturnUnsupported and never came back.  It
spent the rest of the hour re-registering IOAcceleratorES notifications
(279x) while SpringBoard's main thread stayed frozen in the same
mach_msg2/BoardServices wait as in 0104.

0107 answers that one query with zeroed output and kIOReturnSuccess.
Everything else stays refused, so the next log shows the next dependency
one boot at a time.  A zeroed device-info style struct is the least
committal answer; if backboardd dereferences it badly the sampler and
panic screen say so.
---
 iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetPlatformExpert.cpp | 17 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetPlatformExpert.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetPlatformExpert.cpp
index 99ca8d7..49d0e37 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetPlatformExpert.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetPlatformExpert.cpp
@@ -158,6 +158,23 @@ IOAcceleratorESUserClient::externalMethod(uint32_t selector,
     OSObject *target __unused, void *reference __unused)
 {
+	/* 0107: the one query backboardd made in the 0106 boot: sel 2,
+	 * nothing in, 536 bytes out, asked once and abandoned after the
+	 * refusal.  Answer it with zeroed output; every other selector
+	 * stays refused so the next log names the next dependency. */
+	if (args != NULL && selector == 2 &&
+	    args->structureInputSize == 0 &&
+	    args->structureOutputSize == 536 &&
+	    args->structureOutput != NULL) {
+		memset(args->structureOutput, 0, args->structureOutputSize);
+		if (sweet_gpu_uc_logged < 200) {
+			sweet_gpu_uc_logged++;
+			IOLog("SWEET GPU: uc sel 2 out 536 -> zeroed success (shim)\n");
+		}
+		return kIOReturnSuccess;
+	}
 	if (sweet_gpu_uc_logged < 200) {
 		sweet_gpu_uc_logged++;
 		IOLog("SWEET GPU: uc sel %u in %u/%u out %u/%u -> unsupported (shim)\n",
--
